This code uses the `TPL Dataflow` library to implement a pipeline that reads text files from a directory, performs word frequency analysis on each file, and outputs the top N most frequent words for each document along with their respective counts. The program takes an optional command-line argument specifying the number of top words to display (default: 10).

The code defines four `TransformManyBlock`s: one for reading files (`fileReaderBlock`), one for word frequency analysis (`frequencyAnalysisBlock`), one for merging word frequencies across all files (`mergeFrequenciesBlock`), and one for sorting and limiting top words (`sortLimitBlock`). The output block is defined as an `ActionBlock` that outputs the top N most frequent words to the console.

The program connects the blocks using `LinkTo` methods, with `PropagateCompletion = true` options to ensure that completion of one block triggers the execution of subsequent blocks in the pipeline. The program also uses `Task.Run` to perform asynchronous operations on the data and ensures proper error handling for file reading and data processing.

To use this code, simply specify the path to the directory containing your text files as a command-line argument (e.g., ""path/to/files""), and optionally specify the number of top words to display (e.g., 10). The program will read all text files in the specified directory, perform word frequency analysis on each file, and output the top N most frequent words for each document along with their respective counts to the console.']",0,[0],[],True,0,0,0,

